"""M3.2D Daily Stock Summary: one report day, generated by hand, previewed, and saved as HTML + JSON.

The summary is built from ONE consistent read-only view of the local database (history.read_view). It reuses the
stored M3.2C alerts (no quantity rule is re-implemented here) and the M3.1 / M4A observed changes. Nothing is written
to the database, Excel or Drive; Excel is never parsed. M3.2E (scheduling) is meant to call generate() and save() too.

Report day. The day is the computer's local calendar date. Its local start and the next day's local start are
converted to UTC and queried as [start, next start): every snapshot, sync run and evaluation belongs to exactly one
day. A change belongs to the day on which it was OBSERVED (snapshot observed_at = when this program synchronised the
new version), never to the file's modified time and never to an assumed business date.

Verification. A report is VERIFIED only when all of these hold:
- the report day is today;
- this session itself verified both Excel files (its Authorization) and re-checked the alerts (alert_current) at the
  gate generation captured with the request, and that generation is still current after the read;
- the database's current versions are exactly the authorised versions, whose newest sync records are successes of
  the authorised paths;
- the stored alerts come from the latest COMPLETED evaluation of exactly those versions (an evaluation of an older
  version of the same path is never accepted as current).
Anything else is UNVERIFIED and is produced only after the user's explicit confirmation (allow_unverified); the
preview, the file names and the report content then say UNVERIFIED. Cloud Not Verified is always stated.

Immutability. DailySummary carries the canonical JSON text; the HTML is rendered from that same text, so both files
share the report ID, date and version basis. save() stages both files in a private `.partial` directory and makes
the report visible by ONE no-overwrite rename of that directory, after re-checking - under a held database read lock -
that the database basis (versions, newest sync records, evaluation) is still the summary's. A verified summary is a
point-in-time observation of local versions; it never asserts later or cloud freshness.
"""
